--- A function that answers `[turned]` (a one-item list of a local holding a value
--- that can fail) on one path and a list of lists from a call handed that local on
--- another (`return split_up[turned]`) gives a WRONG ANSWER compiled: the second
--- path's list comes back EMPTY.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i):
---     [['c'], ['b'], ['a']]
---     [['a']]
--- Compiled (crow run):
---     []
---     [['a']]
--- Expected: the interpreter's output in both modes.
---
--- `crow build --ir`: `pure pieces(words: List(Str)) -> Result(List(Result(List(Str))))`
--- — `[move turned]` makes the answer a list of values that can fail, and
--- `return call split_up(unwrap move turned)` hands a List(List(Str)) into it. The
--- generated Rust converts that answer by building a new, empty list:
---     return { let _r = { … CrowResult { ok: true, value: split_up(&_prop_0.value), … } };
---              CrowResult { ok: _r.ok, value: Vec::<CrowResult<Vec<String>>>::new(), … } };
--- With `return split_up[words]` (an argument that cannot fail) the program does not
--- build instead (E0308), the conversion missing; so with two one-item lists, one of a
--- local that can fail and one of a local a guard made good (`s = turned[a]`, `c =
--- turned[b]`, `if not c.ok` / `return c`, then `return [s]` and `return [c]`). Found in
--- rosetta/weiler_atherton_polygon_clipping.co: with the clipper's polygons turned into
--- new locals (`s = from_outside[…]`, `return [s]` where nothing crosses, `return
--- walk[…]` otherwise), the compiled program printed "0 polygons" for the task's
--- polygons, where the interpreter (and every other entry) finds 2. Worked around there by
--- unwrapping the turned polygon before it is answered in a list (see the solution).

fn maybe_reversed[words]
    if len[words] gt 9
        return error['too many']
    return reverse[words]

fn split_up[words]
    out = []
    for w in words
        out = push[out, [w]]
    return out

fn pieces[words]
    turned = maybe_reversed[words]
    if len[words] eq 1
        return [turned]
    return split_up[turned]

log[pieces[['a', 'b', 'c']]]
log[pieces[['a']]]
